\section{Validation Methodology}
\label{sec:direction-validation}

The estimator is evaluated on the same directional records used by the marine
motion study: four JONSWAP seas and four Pierson--Moskowitz seas with nonlinear
Stokes harmonics.  Each record is \SI{1200}{s} long at \SI{200}{Hz}; scoring
uses the final \SI{900}{s} so that startup and magnetic-reference acquisition
do not dominate the direction metric.  Ten paired seed triplets vary the wave
realization, sensor noise and drift, and residual-error initialization.  The
direction evidence is simulation based; it is not yet a comparison against a
physical directional wave buoy.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Stationary direction-validation scenarios.}
\label{tab:direction-scenarios}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
Scenario & $H_s$ [m] & $T_p$ [s] & propagation-to azimuth \\
\midrule
W1 & 0.27 & 3.0  & $+30^\circ$ \\
W2 & 1.50 & 5.7  & $-30^\circ$ \\
W3 & 4.00 & 8.5  & $+30^\circ$ \\
W4 & 8.50 & 11.4 & $-30^\circ$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The propagation-plane estimate is axial, so its error is evaluated modulo
$180^\circ$.  For truth axis $\theta$ and estimate $\hat\theta$, define
\begin{equation}
e_{\rm axis}=\frac12\left|\wrap_{(-\pi,\pi]}
\left(2(\hat\theta-\theta)\right)\right|.
\label{eq:axis-error}
\end{equation}
Once travel sense is resolved, the directed propagation-to vector is scored
modulo $360^\circ$.  Travel sense is counted as correct, wrong, or unresolved
against the generator's physical propagation direction.  The implementation's
FORWARD/BACKWARD labels themselves are not treated as physical truth: they are
labels relative to whichever axial representative the estimator returns and
can exchange names when that representative flips by $180^\circ$.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:direction-results}

Across the eight stationary JONSWAP and PM--Stokes seas, the pooled absolute
circular-mean wave-axis error is
\OUValidationDirectionAbsError{} degrees and the worst scenario is
\OUValidationDirectionWorstAbsError{} degrees.  Truth-referenced travel sense
is correct for \OUValidationTravelCorrect\% of samples, wrong for
\OUValidationTravelWrong\%, and unresolved for
\OUValidationTravelUnresolved\%.  The absolute error of the final directed
estimate is \OUValidationTravelAbsError{} degrees under the aggregate metric
reported by the committed validation bundle.

These numbers answer two different questions.  The axial statistic measures
whether horizontal orbital motion identifies the vertical propagation plane;
the travel-sense statistic measures whether the horizontal--vertical phase test
chooses the correct direction along that plane.  A low axial error therefore
does not, by itself, demonstrate a correct $0$--$360^\circ$ propagation
direction, which is why both stages are scored separately.  Figures
\ref{fig:wave-dir-jonswap-medium}--\ref{fig:wave-dir-pmstokes-medium} show the
same distinction in the generated time-domain diagnostics: direction angle,
uncertainty and confidence are estimator metrics, while the final panel is the
separate travel-sense decision.

